WEBVTT
Kind: captions
Language: en

00:00:00.800 --> 00:00:02.368
-♪ "A to Z" ♪

00:00:02.368 --> 00:00:03.802
♪ "A to Z" ♪

00:00:03.802 --> 00:00:05.171
-Explain it to me.
-All right. Here's how it works.

00:00:05.171 --> 00:00:06.705
You and I are going to have
a conversation

00:00:06.705 --> 00:00:08.040
about our favorite music.

00:00:08.040 --> 00:00:09.975
But the catch is that each time
one of us brings up

00:00:09.975 --> 00:00:12.879
a new band or musician,
their name has to start with

00:00:12.879 --> 00:00:14.380
the next letter of
the alphabet.

00:00:14.380 --> 00:00:15.748
We'll go back and forth
to see if you can get

00:00:15.748 --> 00:00:17.484
all the way from A to Z.

00:00:17.484 --> 00:00:19.452
-Oh, this is fun.
-All right, ready for this?

00:00:19.452 --> 00:00:21.020
All right, so,
Rami, "Bohemian Rhapsody"

00:00:21.020 --> 00:00:22.288
is a fun movie to make.

00:00:22.288 --> 00:00:23.756
You must have
grew up with music.

00:00:23.756 --> 00:00:25.157
-I did.

00:00:25.157 --> 00:00:28.828
One of the first bands I heard
was AC/DC, from Australia.

00:00:28.828 --> 00:00:32.465
Do you know them?
[ Cheers and applause ]

00:00:32.465 --> 00:00:33.833
-I know them a little bit.

00:00:33.833 --> 00:00:36.002
I grew up more with the Beastie
Boys and stuff like that.

00:00:36.002 --> 00:00:38.504
-Yeah. I love the Beastie boys.
-You do, right?

00:00:38.504 --> 00:00:39.872
Yeah, it's good music.

00:00:39.872 --> 00:00:41.307
-Yeah, great, great --

00:00:41.307 --> 00:00:43.676
This is already going to be
very interesting, isn't it?

00:00:43.676 --> 00:00:44.677
-Yeah.

00:00:44.677 --> 00:00:47.213
-Oh, speaking of
Beastie Boys,

00:00:47.213 --> 00:00:50.149
This is probably just
a total non sequitur,

00:00:50.149 --> 00:00:54.420
but Cat Stevens is --
-"Speaking of Beastie Boys..."

00:00:54.420 --> 00:00:56.389
-I mean talk
about guys who just

00:00:56.389 --> 00:00:59.592
completely reinvented
themselves, because he --

00:00:59.592 --> 00:01:01.027
-David Bowie.
-Yes.

00:01:01.027 --> 00:01:02.795
-Yeah. Great example.
-Great example.

00:01:02.795 --> 00:01:04.096
-Yeah, it really is.

00:01:04.096 --> 00:01:06.565
-The song with Queen,
"Under Pressure," is incredible.

00:01:06.565 --> 00:01:07.733
-It is a good song.

00:01:07.733 --> 00:01:09.502
-And then you have another one

00:01:09.502 --> 00:01:12.738
of their contemporaries,
Elton John.

00:01:12.738 --> 00:01:14.173
-Oh, we love Elton John.
-Right?

00:01:14.173 --> 00:01:16.542
Sure.
-He's up there with Fergie.

00:01:16.542 --> 00:01:18.144
-Fergie.

00:01:18.144 --> 00:01:22.081
And the Goo Goo Dolls
could just be --

00:01:22.081 --> 00:01:24.050
they could be right up there,
as well, right?

00:01:24.050 --> 00:01:29.222
-Well, yeah they can.
You know who's underrated?

00:01:29.222 --> 00:01:32.458
Herman's Hermits.
-Yes.

00:01:32.458 --> 00:01:33.993
-Underrated.
-Very, very.

00:01:33.993 --> 00:01:35.695
-Because, yeah, you just don't
hear enough of them,

00:01:35.695 --> 00:01:36.729
and I like their songs.

00:01:36.729 --> 00:01:38.063
They're all good.
-I think so.

00:01:38.063 --> 00:01:41.234
And, you know, when you think
about Herman's Hermits

00:01:41.234 --> 00:01:46.306
and Freddie Mercury, another guy
who ripped his shirt off

00:01:46.306 --> 00:01:49.508
quite a bit was Iggy Pop, right?

00:01:49.508 --> 00:01:51.210
-Did the Herman's Hermits
rip their shirts off?

00:01:51.210 --> 00:01:53.479
-You've never seen
Herman's Hermits...

00:01:53.479 --> 00:01:54.747
-No, I've never seen that.

00:01:54.747 --> 00:01:56.749
Everyone I've enjoyed
always wore their clothes,

00:01:56.749 --> 00:01:57.983
including the Jackson 5.

00:01:57.983 --> 00:01:59.585
Everyone always
had everything on.

00:01:59.585 --> 00:02:01.187
That's the way I like my music.
-Yes, they did.

00:02:01.187 --> 00:02:02.355
-Yeah.

00:02:02.355 --> 00:02:05.491
-When you have "K" and...
-What's that?

00:02:05.491 --> 00:02:06.892
Are you talking about --

00:02:06.892 --> 00:02:11.564
-Yes, the -- I can think of
The Killers at this moment.

00:02:11.564 --> 00:02:12.698
-Yeah.

00:02:12.698 --> 00:02:14.400
-But how am I going
to incorporate this

00:02:14.400 --> 00:02:16.435
into a sentence to keep going?

00:02:16.435 --> 00:02:20.840
You just keep going,
why don't you?

00:02:20.840 --> 00:02:22.941
-You know, I wish there
was rock festivals

00:02:22.941 --> 00:02:24.143
when I was growing --

00:02:24.143 --> 00:02:26.179
like, from back in the day
that I got to see,

00:02:26.179 --> 00:02:28.281
'cause I would love to see
Little Richard.

00:02:28.281 --> 00:02:29.448
-Little Richard.

00:02:29.448 --> 00:02:31.083
Oh, I mean --

00:02:31.083 --> 00:02:32.452
-Who else
would be on the bill?

00:02:32.452 --> 00:02:35.921
-I think, you know, anyone that
could sing like a virgin.

00:02:35.921 --> 00:02:38.490
Madonna would be great.
-I'd Love to see -- She's great.

00:02:38.490 --> 00:02:42.361
-Yeah.
-So is Nas.

00:02:42.361 --> 00:02:45.564
-Nas.
Nas was my youth, man.

00:02:45.564 --> 00:02:48.000
-Madonna or Nas?
-And Nas.

00:02:48.000 --> 00:02:49.635
But if we really want to
throw it back,

00:02:49.635 --> 00:02:52.104
I think Nas really loved
Otis Redding.

00:02:52.104 --> 00:02:53.940
Did you know that?
-Really?

00:02:53.940 --> 00:02:55.041
-Yeah.

00:02:55.041 --> 00:02:59.645
-He didn't like
Peter, Paul and Mary?

00:02:59.645 --> 00:03:02.014
I mean, what's his problem?
-No.

00:03:02.014 --> 00:03:03.048
-What's his problem?

00:03:03.048 --> 00:03:08.053
-He was also a big fan of
the obvious,

00:03:08.053 --> 00:03:11.624
Questlove.

00:03:11.624 --> 00:03:16.162
You can not talk about Questlove
without talking about --

00:03:16.162 --> 00:03:18.631
-Yeah, without Questlove,
there would be no Roots.

00:03:18.631 --> 00:03:21.334
-Yes!

00:03:21.334 --> 00:03:24.570
And without Roots,
there would be no soul.

00:03:24.570 --> 00:03:25.905
And that's not a band,

00:03:25.905 --> 00:03:30.576
but I'm going to
keep it going, baby.

00:03:30.576 --> 00:03:32.245
-Yeah.
-Yeah.

00:03:32.245 --> 00:03:33.780
-Yeah.

00:03:33.780 --> 00:03:36.949
-Or Nina Simone is someone that
they definitely drew from, yes?

00:03:36.949 --> 00:03:39.118
-Well, Nina Simone,
but, I mean, he plays drums.

00:03:39.118 --> 00:03:40.653
Tito Puente,
you know, stuff like that.

00:03:40.653 --> 00:03:42.455
-Oh, it's all full circle.

00:03:42.455 --> 00:03:46.158
And, of course, you know,
a band that is iconic

00:03:46.158 --> 00:03:51.330
for playing everything
together is U2.

00:03:51.330 --> 00:03:52.965
-They're known for
playing things together.

00:03:52.965 --> 00:03:54.199
That is correct.

00:03:54.199 --> 00:03:56.135
-Yes, they are a band
that plays instruments.

00:03:56.135 --> 00:03:57.870
-"Do you guys want to see
people play together?

00:03:57.870 --> 00:03:59.072
You've got to go check out U2."

00:03:59.072 --> 00:04:00.839
-They are the ones.
-Yeah, or The Verve Pipe.

00:04:00.839 --> 00:04:02.875
-The Verve Pipe, yeah.

00:04:02.875 --> 00:04:06.245
Or...Who?

00:04:06.245 --> 00:04:09.282
[ Laughter ]

00:04:09.282 --> 00:04:11.317
[ Cheers and applause ]

00:04:19.124 --> 00:04:21.961
-Who is a good question.
-Who is a good question.

00:04:21.961 --> 00:04:26.065
-Yeah, because
there's a great punk band

00:04:26.065 --> 00:04:27.700
with John Doe called X.

00:04:27.700 --> 00:04:29.835
-Yes. Punk-rock band.
-The Punk-rock band.

00:04:29.835 --> 00:04:34.673
-Yes. And back to that band
that plays everything together,

00:04:34.673 --> 00:04:37.476
if you spell out the name
of the band,

00:04:37.476 --> 00:04:43.950
it would be You2, Y-O-U.

00:04:43.950 --> 00:04:47.420
[ Cymbal crash ]

00:04:47.420 --> 00:04:50.889
-You want to go get some beers
and go see ZZ Top?

00:04:50.889 --> 00:04:55.461
-I love ZZ Top! Yeah!

00:04:55.461 --> 00:04:58.097
-That's what I'm talking about.
Rami Malek, everybody!

